KANBAN TASK WORKER

Workspace: {{workspace}}
Kanban path: {{kanban_path}}
Task id: {{task_id}}
Task policy: {{task_policy}}
Validation command: {{validation_command}}
Max workers: {{max_workers}}
Pipeline step: {{pipeline_step}}
Previous results: {{previous_results}}

Work on exactly one task. Read the kanban file, parse tasks in the ## TASKS section, and select the requested task. If task_id is AUTO, select the first pending task whose dependencies are all [x]. A dependency in [P], [=], [ ], [*], or [N] is not satisfied.

Before editing, inspect the task Description, Scope, Out of Scope, Acceptance Criteria, project specs, and existing code patterns. Implement the task in the workspace. Do not use git add, git commit, git reset, git restore, or git clean. If validation_command is not none, run it after implementation.

Max workers is the parallelism budget the operator granted for this run. When it is greater than 1, you MAY fan out independent, non-conflicting subtasks within that budget, but each run still reports a single primary task result. When it is 1, work strictly sequentially.

Update the kanban checkbox only when appropriate: [=] while working, [P] when implementation is ready for review, [*] if blocked or failed. Do not mark [x] unless the task is already merged or the operator explicitly requested completion without PR review.

Write a RESULT file at the workspace root containing valid JSON with this shape:
{
  "signal": "ready_for_review|blocked|failed|no_task",
  "summary": "one concise sentence",
  "task_id": "TASK-001",
  "status": "pending_approval|blocked|failed|none",
  "changed_files": [],
  "validation": {"command": "...", "status": "passed|failed|skipped", "summary": "..."},
  "evidence": []
}

If no eligible task exists, do not edit files; write signal no_task.
